# 6. Repository Safety Rules

Codex must never:

- Rewrite Git history
- Force-push without explicit approval
- Delete branches without explicit approval
- Delete user work because it appears unused
- Overwrite a file without first reading its current content
- Commit secrets
- Commit generated credentials
- Commit personal network identifiers
- Commit large binary assets without confirming they belong in source control
- Change signing, provisioning or bundle identity casually
- Modify entitlements without explaining the requirement
- Add third-party dependencies without approval

For meaningful code changes, Codex should normally work on a feature branch unless Daniel explicitly requests direct changes to `main`.

Documentation-only additions may be committed directly when explicitly requested.

Commit messages should be:

- Specific
- Imperative or descriptive
- Limited to one logical change

Good:

```text
Add automation trigger deduplication
Fix Hue event stream reconnect state
Document HomeKit authorization boundary
```

Avoid:

```text
Updates
Fix stuff
Changes
WIP final final
```

