# 11. UI and SwiftUI Rules

## 11.1 Design System First

All UI must use Aura design tokens and reusable components.

Do not hardcode:

- Colors
- Spacing
- Corner radii
- Typography
- Animation durations
- Shadows
- Haptic mappings

When a required token does not exist, Codex should propose a token addition rather than inventing a one-off value.

## 11.2 Component Reuse

Before creating a component, Codex must check `08_Component_Library` and existing code.

If three screens need the same pattern, it should become a reusable Aura component.

Do not create multiple nearly identical cards with different names.

## 11.3 View Complexity

A SwiftUI view should remain focused on composition.

Extract a subview when it:

- Has its own responsibility
- Has independent state or accessibility behavior
- Is reused
- Makes the parent materially easier to understand

Do not split every few lines into a new type merely to reduce file length.

## 11.4 State Management

View-local ephemeral state belongs in the view.

Feature state belongs in a feature store or view model.

Domain state belongs in domain owners.

Never duplicate confirmed device state inside multiple screens.

## 11.5 Previews

Reusable visual components and meaningful screen states should include previews when practical.

Previews must use mocks and must not access:

- Local network
- Keychain
- HomeKit
- Real devices
- Production persistence

## 11.6 Loading and Errors

Prefer:

- Cached content
- Skeletons
- Progressive updates
- Inline recovery

Avoid blank screens and blocking spinners.

User-facing errors must describe:

- What happened
- What the user can do next

Raw errors belong in diagnostics, not normal UI.

---

# 12. Accessibility Rules

Accessibility is required for every user-facing change.

Codex must verify:

- VoiceOver labels
- VoiceOver values
- VoiceOver hints where useful
- Logical reading order
- Dynamic Type
- Minimum touch target
- Sufficient contrast
- Reduce Motion
- Non-color state indicators
- Focus stability during live updates

Controls must announce state separately from action.

Example:

```text
Label: Living room lights
Value: On, 40 percent brightness
Hint: Double tap to turn off
```

Haptics and motion must never be the only feedback.

Security-sensitive actions must clearly announce pending and confirmed states.

---

# 13. Motion and Haptics Rules

Motion must explain change.

Haptics must reinforce meaningful interaction.

Codex must use the systems defined in:

- `10_Motion_System`
- `11_Haptics`

Do not:

- Add decorative continuous animation
- Trigger haptics for background events
- Stack multiple haptics for one action
- Fake scene completion through animation
- Delay interaction until an animation finishes
- Ignore Reduce Motion

Scene progress must reflect real execution state.

---

# 14. Networking Rules

All network clients must define:

- Request timeout
- Cancellation behavior
- Response-size limits where relevant
- Error translation
- Logging category
- Retry classification
- Security assumptions

Codex must not implement:

- Broad port scanning
- Infinite retries
- Aggressive polling
- Credentials in URLs
- Secrets in logs
- Silent HTTP fallback where HTTPS is required
- Trust-all certificate handling

Retries are allowed only when the operation is safe to retry.

Operations that may already have succeeded must not be repeated blindly.

Event-driven updates are preferred.

Polling must be bounded, contextual and stopped when no longer necessary.

---

# 15. Vendor Integration Rules

## 15.1 General

Before implementing a vendor capability, Codex must verify:

- Public API availability
- Authentication requirements
- Platform permissions
- Current protocol version
- Device or firmware limitations
- App Store implications

Capability support must come from verified runtime data, not product-name assumptions.

Unsupported capabilities must remain absent.

Do not simulate support.

## 15.2 Philips TV

Codex must not assume that endpoints from another Philips model apply to `65PUS7800/12`.

Model- and firmware-specific behavior must be verified.

Power and standby behavior require real-device validation.

## 15.3 Apple TV

Codex must not ship private or reverse-engineered Apple TV protocols without an explicit approved decision.

Desired functionality in documentation is not proof of a public Apple API.

## 15.4 HomeKit

HomeKit framework objects must remain inside the HomeKit integration boundary.

Codex must respect current entitlements, authorization behavior and public framework limits.

## 15.5 Philips Hue

Hue API versions must remain isolated.

Hue API v2 production communication must use HTTPS.

Normal REST control must respect current bridge command-rate guidance.

High-frequency entertainment streaming must use the dedicated entertainment path, not repeated REST commands.

