# 16. Security Rules

Codex must treat security as a design requirement.

Secrets include:

- API keys
- Pairing tokens
- Authentication headers
- Home identifiers where sensitive
- Raw device serial numbers
- Private network details

Secrets must:

- Be stored in Keychain or approved secure storage
- Never appear in logs
- Never appear in analytics
- Never be committed
- Never be embedded in source code
- Never be copied into test fixtures without sanitization

All external input is untrusted.

Codex must use:

- Strict decoding
- Input validation
- Bounded payloads
- Safe string rendering
- Explicit trust decisions

Do not weaken transport security to make development easier.

Development-only bypasses require clear compile-time isolation and must never be enabled in release builds.

---

# 17. Privacy Rules

Aura is local-first.

Codex must not add data upload, analytics or cloud storage implicitly.

The following data should remain local by default:

- Device names
- Room names
- Scene names
- Automation definitions
- Local IP addresses
- Device usage
- Motion events
- Viewing activity
- HomeKit structure
- Diagnostic history

New telemetry requires:

- Explicit product approval
- Documented purpose
- Data minimization
- User consent where required
- Redaction rules
- Retention rules

Crash and diagnostic exports must be sanitized.

User-created names must not be included in analytics by default.

---

# 18. Persistence Rules

Persistence must use versioned models.

Codex shall define:

- Stable identifiers
- Schema version
- Migration path
- Failure recovery
- Backup behavior where relevant

Cached state is not automatically authoritative.

Security-sensitive current state must never be restored as confirmed solely from cache.

Examples include:

- Lock status
- Alarm state
- Garage state
- Presence state
- Current authorization

Credentials must not be stored alongside ordinary Codable data.

Destructive migrations require explicit approval and backup consideration.

---

# 19. Diagnostics and Logging Rules

Every subsystem capable of failure must integrate with Diagnostics.

Codex should add:

- Structured log category
- Correlation ID where an operation crosses modules
- Start and completion events for meaningful operations
- Sanitized error context
- Recovery outcome

Examples of correlation boundaries:

- Device discovery session
- Pairing flow
- Scene run
- Automation run
- Diagnostic test
- Reconnection attempt

Logs must not include secrets or unredacted private identifiers.

Normal UI should show actionable language.

Advanced diagnostics may show technical detail after sanitization.

Do not use `print()` as production logging.

---

# 20. Error Handling Rules

Errors must remain scoped whenever possible.

One unavailable light must not mark the entire Hue Bridge unusable.

One failed automation action must not erase successful action results.

Codex should distinguish:

- Validation error
- Authorization error
- Unavailable resource
- Timeout
- Cancellation
- Transient network failure
- Permanent unsupported capability
- Partial success
- Unknown system failure

Do not convert cancellation into an error banner.

Do not retry authorization denial indefinitely.

Do not expose raw framework or vendor errors to users.

Every recoverable error should define a recovery action.

---

# 21. Scene and Automation Rules

## 21.1 Scene Engine

Scene execution must remain owned by the Scene Engine.

Codex must not implement scene orchestration inside:

- A SwiftUI view
- A device plugin
- An automation trigger handler

Scene actions require:

- Stable action identity
- Timeout behavior
- Cancellation behavior
- Result reporting
- Correlation ID
- Retry classification

Parallel actions may run concurrently only when their dependencies allow it.

## 21.2 Automation Engine

Automation evaluation must be deterministic.

Codex must preserve:

- Event deduplication
- Cooldown
- Debounce
- Loop prevention
- Correlation tracking
- Execution history
- Reliability classification

An automation must not recursively trigger itself through reflected device state.

Background execution must remain honest about iOS limitations.

Do not promise exact unattended execution unless the platform can provide it.

---

# 22. Testing Rules

No meaningful production logic should be added without a test strategy.

## 22.1 Unit Tests

Unit tests should cover:

- Domain policies
- Mapping
- Validation
- State transitions
- Retry classification
- Deduplication
- Error translation
- Persistence migration

## 22.2 Integration Tests

Integration tests should cover boundaries such as:

- Plugin to registry
- Registry to application use case
- Scene Engine to plugins
- Automation Engine to Scene Engine
- Diagnostics provider aggregation

## 22.3 UI Tests

UI tests should focus on high-value flows:

- First launch
- Add device
- Run scene
- Create automation
- Recover from offline device
- Open diagnostics

Avoid brittle tests that assert incidental animation timing or exact view hierarchy.

## 22.4 Hardware Tests

Real hardware is mandatory for capabilities that cannot be proven in simulation.

Initial hardware matrix:

- iPhone 16
- Philips TV 65PUS7800/12
- Apple TV 4K first generation
- Philips Hue Bridge v2
- HomePod mini
- HomeKit environment

Codex must clearly mark hardware-required validation.

## 22.5 Test Quality

Tests must:

- Be deterministic
- Avoid real secrets
- Avoid relying on public internet availability
- Use injectable clocks for time behavior
- Use mocks or fixtures for vendor responses
- Verify behavior rather than implementation details

Do not weaken a test merely to make it pass.

